  "query_preview": ".. \n\nEarly afternoon satellite imagery shows an upper low spinning off\nthe CA coast near 130W, while a fetch of low-mid level southerly \nflow is beginning to pull tropical moisture from the Baja region \nnorthward. As this upstream low begins to move inland, this\nmoisture will quickly spread northward into southern Utah by \nWednesday morning, and into central Utah by afternoon, bringing \nthe potential for heavy rainfall and flash flooding. This moisture\nwill remain in place through Thursday, resulting in a relatively \nlong-duration window of potentially heavy rainfall across central \nand southern Utah, where storm totals may locally exceed 5\" in \nupslope-favored areas such as the Pine Valleys and areas near \nBrian Head. As such have gone ahead with a Flood Watch for much of\nsouthern and eastern Utah for Wednesday through Thursday evening.\n\n\nAs the area comes under the influence of the upstream low\nWednesday, enhanced low and deep layer shear combined with low\nLCLs will support supercells capable of all severe weather hazards\nassuming sufficient instability is able to develop. This appears\nmost likely to occur across southwest Utah in closer proximity to\nthe upper dynamics. This threat will persist into Thursday and \nexpand northward into central and perhaps northern Utah assuming\nsufficient instability. \n\nThe upper low is forecast to lift through the forecast area\nThursday night into Friday, focusing precipitation across northern\nUtah and southwest Wyoming. As colder air spreads into the area,\nsnow levels will fall to around 8500 feet with accumulating snow\nlikely above 9000 feet. Snow accumulations of 2-6 inches above \n9000 feet look likely, with the potential for higher totals \ndepending on how the upper low evolves as it lifts through the \nregion. A secondary trough digging into the region may keep \nprecipitation going into the weekend, although confidence is \nfairly low at this time with respect to how the overall pattern \nevolves.",
